# Automatic Table of Contents in LaTeX

Here's a complete example demonstrating how to generate an automatic table of contents in LaTeX:

```latex
\documentclass{article}

% Optional: Customize ToC depth (default is 3)
\setcounter{tocdepth}{3}

\title{Sample Document with Table of Contents}
\author{Author Name}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

% Generate the Table of Contents
\tableofcontents

% Optional: Start new page after ToC
\newpage

% Document sections - these will appear in ToC automatically
\section{Introduction}
This is the introduction to our document.

\subsection{Background}
Some background information goes here.

\subsection{Objectives}
The objectives of this document are listed here.

\section{Methodology}
This section describes the methodology used.

\subsection{Data Collection}
Information about data collection.

\subsubsection{Primary Sources}
Details about primary sources.

\subsubsection{Secondary Sources}
Details about secondary sources.

\subsection{Analysis Techniques}
Description of analysis techniques.

\section{Results}
The results are presented in this section.

\section{Conclusion}
Final conclusions and remarks.

\end{document}
```

## Key Commands

| Command | Purpose |
|---------|---------|
| `\tableofcontents` | Generates the table of contents |
| `\section{}` | Creates a numbered section (Level 1) |
| `\subsection{}` | Creates a numbered subsection (Level 2) |
| `\subsubsection{}` | Creates a numbered subsubsection (Level 3) |
| `\setcounter{tocdepth}{n}` | Sets how many levels appear in ToC |

## Important Note

**Compile twice!** LaTeX requires two compilations:
1. First compilation collects section information
2. Second compilation generates the actual ToC

